[SPEAKER_00]: them or working with them you wouldn't have had all of this come together and look like this nice tidy package that other people will buy and that

28:58.566 --> 29:10.486
[SPEAKER_00]: you yourself as an athlete and a filmmaker you buy because you can see how beautiful it is right Got it, man. I'm so glad I used the word masterclass

29:10.506 --> 29:18.167
[SPEAKER_02]: for this because, of course, you gave the blueprint on how to do things, and you also gave the blueprint in the second part of what to do when

29:18.207 --> 29:26.729
[SPEAKER_02]: things don't go right. So moving forward, I would want to come to the most important part of putting together a project, which I'm pretty sure a

29:26.989 --> 29:36.494
[SPEAKER_02]: lot of our audience members are going to be curious about, and that is filming in flight. I would love to have you talk over what kind of camera

29:36.514 --> 29:47.185
[SPEAKER_02]: gear you shoot, what kind of shot selections do you do. Because to me, when GoPro and all the mounts and the selfie sticks came across, they

29:47.225 --> 29:55.591
[SPEAKER_02]: looked like a pretty big safety hazard for me. where they could snag lines and one collapse could take away your distraction and send you

29:55.651 --> 30:01.716
[SPEAKER_02]: scrambling for handling the selfie stick and everything. Of course, it has evolved over a period of time and now cameras are shooting in 8K,

30:01.756 --> 30:09.562
[SPEAKER_02]: which can fit in your pocket and everything. How have you navigated this aspect of filming in flight and what's your usual go-to setup in making

30:09.842 --> 30:18.769
[SPEAKER_02]: all the possible magical shots come together? Sure. It's a big subject. Because...

30:20.408 --> 30:32.333
[SPEAKER_00]: All right. Filming yourself in general, like nevermind paragliding, because it applies to paragliding. for me, sorry, I'll say filming

30:32.373 --> 30:45.196
[SPEAKER_00]: myself requires a lot of self-discipline because so often I'm, at least ideally, I'm doing something that I would be doing if the camera wasn't

30:45.236 --> 30:52.858
[SPEAKER_00]: there. And so that means that I'm just excited about what it is that I'm doing. And at some point, when you're trying to create hundreds of hours

30:52.898 --> 31:03.857
[SPEAKER_00]: of footage to be able to edit it down into one hour, an hour and a half of really great footage, you got to get the camera out a lot. And so you might

31:04.537 --> 31:13.300
[SPEAKER_00]: plan on walking up this mountain and averaging five kilometers an hour. But the truth is, if you want to be able to present that to people later,

31:13.360 --> 31:22.022
[SPEAKER_00]: you have to plan on moving at maybe more like two kilometers an hour because of all the time it takes to run ahead, get a tripod out, get the

31:22.062 --> 31:25.723
[SPEAKER_00]: camera running, make sure the batteries are good, get the audio going. And so...

31:28.721 --> 31:39.770
[SPEAKER_00]: That takes a lot of self-discipline for me to slow myself down, to create this stuff. And it especially takes that self-discipline when I'm not

31:39.810 --> 31:46.355
[SPEAKER_00]: feeling great. Because when I'm not feeling great, I don't have that skip in my step. Like maybe it's raining. Maybe I just had a bad flight.

31:46.855 --> 31:56.983
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe I'm really tired. Maybe I'm super dehydrated. That's happened. And all I can do is think about getting to the next potential lake or

31:57.043 --> 32:06.724
[SPEAKER_00]: stream. But I have to realize that those moments where I'm really not doing well, those are vital in filmmaking because I

32:09.745 --> 32:18.529
[SPEAKER_00]: tend to get the camera out when I'm doing great and I just love the world and I love myself and I love my project and I wanna share it. But without

32:18.949 --> 32:31.696
[SPEAKER_00]: the sad, awkward, difficult, painful moments in between, that becomes very redundant. in it in an edited piece and so that's the first thing is

32:31.736 --> 32:40.964
[SPEAKER_00]: just to recognize like hey it should be really easy in theory but in practice it's just actually really hard and it requires a kind of

32:41.004 --> 32:53.566
[SPEAKER_00]: self-discipline that I barely have and often don't so now moving on to paragliding It can be dangerous to be preoccupied with cameras. It can

32:53.586 --> 33:03.528
[SPEAKER_00]: be dangerous to have things strapped to us that can impact our lines or our reserve system or whatever. So a lot of that thought needs to be put

33:03.569 --> 33:16.280
[SPEAKER_00]: into that. And I feel like safety is top notch, not top notch, is of the highest order. And it's important to find things that Partly for the

33:16.300 --> 33:26.763
[SPEAKER_00]: reasons of that if things are hard, we won't do them like I was talking about on the ground. But also if things require too much time or thought,

33:26.943 --> 33:35.766
[SPEAKER_00]: they're not really safe when you're dangling from 30 string above a mountain. You need to have them be really easy. And you need to be able to

33:35.846 --> 33:45.470
[SPEAKER_00]: do them blindfolded. So as far as filming while paragliding, coming up with creative ideas that maybe you haven't seen before, or maybe you

33:45.510 --> 33:55.659
[SPEAKER_00]: have, but that are really easy to achieve. Like you can do them in 10 seconds, 15 seconds. In terms of setting them up, often I don't launch

33:55.839 --> 34:04.891
[SPEAKER_00]: with my cameras out. I have to release them once I'm paragliding. That's really important is to have things that you can do quickly so that you

34:04.911 --> 34:14.594
[SPEAKER_00]: don't, it doesn't require a ton of self-discipline to get them out and then likely bring them back in before you land. But also that don't

34:14.634 --> 34:21.816
[SPEAKER_00]: require a ton of time because you don't usually have that much time. I usually don't have that much time where I feel like I can fly without

34:21.876 --> 34:31.036
[SPEAKER_00]: having my hands on my toggles and be completely in the game as far as, okay, where's my bomb out? Where's my next thermal, right? Yeah, that

34:31.076 --> 34:38.798
[SPEAKER_02]: makes a lot of sense, I think. Have you been just using action cams for this or have you ever switched to what is a single lens DSLR or something

34:38.818 --> 34:46.020
[SPEAKER_02]: like that? Or what kind of hardware do you usually use to tell your stories as the technology has evolved? Okay, yeah, sure. I can talk

34:46.040 --> 35:00.724
[SPEAKER_00]: about that. So on my last... film that I've released so far. Fly Monarcha, I have a, it's the Sony RX100 fourth edition I used. I've

35:00.764 --> 35:13.828
[SPEAKER_00]: actually been using that one for almost 10 years. I keep buying the same one because I break them. I wear them out. They shoot 4K. They are like

35:14.329 --> 35:24.817
[SPEAKER_00]: mini DSLR. They have a lot of manual options. It's not the best that you're ever going to see, but it's... way better than a phone or at least

35:24.837 --> 35:36.208
[SPEAKER_00]: phones at the time or GoPro or what have you. So that in combination with the tripod is how I do, I would say 75% of my terrestrial stuff. The rest

35:36.248 --> 35:45.758
[SPEAKER_00]: would be using GoPros on selfie sticks, gimbals and stuff like that. And then in the sky, it's pretty much entirely GoPro footage with the

35:45.798 --> 35:53.647
[SPEAKER_00]: exception of As you'll see in Fly Monarcha and a little bit in the Endless Chain, but really in Fly Monarcha, where I had the privilege of having my

35:54.008 --> 36:06.491
[SPEAKER_00]: now wife accompany me as a filmmaker on her own autonomously in a van, she would hike up and capture a lot of very helpful drone footage of me. So I

36:06.511 --> 36:17.376
[SPEAKER_00]: don't want to forget to say that. But as far as me, that was separate from what I was doing, right? So what I'm doing is GoPros. I rely on three shots

36:18.777 --> 36:29.902
[SPEAKER_00]: that I really like that I can set up pretty quick. The easiest is a 360 cam that I jam. So like the GoPro 360, whatever it's called, I just jam that

36:30.042 --> 36:42.575
[SPEAKER_00]: into the flip plate. That's the quickest one. I have another one on a arm that articulates by two hinges, so it's got three sections. And then on

36:42.595 --> 36:51.138
[SPEAKER_00]: that, there's a gimbal and then another GoPro that's shooting me, but I can also turn the gimbal around to shoot forward or to the side. Both of

36:51.198 --> 37:01.641
[SPEAKER_00]: those I can activate by hand in terms of starting and stopping them. And then finally, the most complex one for me to get out is a, let's just call

37:01.661 --> 37:10.242
[SPEAKER_00]: it a follow cam. I think that would be the... accepted term for that which i keep in my harness in the side pocket and then once i feel very

37:10.302 --> 37:18.745
[SPEAKER_00]: comfortable and i'm at some point in the flight that feels like it really needs to be documented that way i'll release that and that'll fly behind

37:18.785 --> 37:28.842
[SPEAKER_00]: me usually on the left side it's biased towards the left wing tip is where i'm comfortable with it and i'll pay that out after I've launched. And

37:28.882 --> 37:36.607
[SPEAKER_00]: then I'll, I actually have a thing that I can bring it back in when I'm landing, because sometimes you don't want something like that out

37:36.627 --> 37:42.812
[SPEAKER_00]: there when you're landing, partly because it'll break, but mostly because I'm terrified of having it get caught on a branch or something

37:42.832 --> 37:51.978
[SPEAKER_00]: like that. And then causing a deflation. That one runs on a, I think it uses Bluetooth, it's either wifi or Bluetooth. I'm not sure, but it's

37:51.998 --> 38:01.481
[SPEAKER_00]: just like the standard GoPro remote that you can, I have that on my cockpit. And so those are the three shots for me, plus whatever my wife

38:01.821 --> 38:13.694
[SPEAKER_00]: was able to capture on our last film with the drone. And then also audio. So the same little... It's called the Zoom H1. I don't know if they make

38:13.714 --> 38:23.837
[SPEAKER_00]: them anymore. Maybe it's H1N. But... anything I was is better than what you can get on your camera. It's just a little recorder. I'll have a lapel

38:23.877 --> 38:35.562
[SPEAKER_00]: mic with a little dead cat. They call it hairball that goes over the mic to cut down on wind. And I'll just clip that to either my buff or my inside of

38:35.602 --> 38:42.925
[SPEAKER_00]: my jacket or something like that. And the sound is a little bit muffled, but I feel like that's a part of the authenticity of the shot. And so when

38:42.965 --> 38:55.763
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm setting up my shots and, let's just say I get three out of three cameras running for a shot, I'll say, okay, flying over Mount Nemo, And

38:55.783 --> 39:04.265
[SPEAKER_00]: then I'll just give a three clap and then that'll sync up all of my cameras and my audio. And then I'll be able to edit that later on. And I'll just

39:04.305 --> 39:14.267
[SPEAKER_00]: talk about something. And ironically, usually I don't use any of the talking because it's just garbage, but it's there in case I do need it.

39:14.367 --> 39:23.066
[SPEAKER_00]: And sometimes I do use it. And when it is good, it's really good. And that pretty much, that's the secret sauce. Yeah. I love it, man. I think

39:23.186 --> 39:29.369
[SPEAKER_02]: sometimes the emotions that come out during the flight are one of the most authentic ones. So I think, as you said, when it's good, it's really

39:29.409 --> 39:37.231
[SPEAKER_02]: good. It's the way to go. But Benjamin, what is the thing that makes you pull back the chase cam? What kind of mechanism do you use for that?

39:37.291 --> 39:44.174
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I've been using chase cam myself, but once I release it, I just basically have to wait until it goes to landing. Can you tell me

39:44.194 --> 39:56.413
[SPEAKER_02]: something about that? Are you? Yeah. Okay. For the Endless Chain, which is the following film, I developed a two-part system. The most

39:56.473 --> 40:07.317
[SPEAKER_00]: important part of it was I created a pulley system where now I, instead of just having the string, the fishing line run from the paraglider down to

40:07.357 --> 40:16.392
[SPEAKER_00]: the chase cam, I have it run up to essentially what is just like a key ring or a little carabiner, like a baby carabiner on the paraglider. But I

40:16.452 --> 40:29.746
[SPEAKER_00]: recommend using a key loop so that nothing can clip into it. And then that runs back down to my own carabiner from my paraglider. And I can release

40:29.906 --> 40:41.649
[SPEAKER_00]: the camera. And then I can... pull that pulley and I pull it up, let's just say five, six, seven, eight meters. And then I attach a little pre-made

40:41.730 --> 40:51.612
[SPEAKER_00]: loop on that fishing string that all of a sudden I see the loop and I attach that. Now, all of a sudden my camera is way higher than it was when I

40:51.652 --> 41:02.376
[SPEAKER_00]: released it. And it's way higher than it would be able to be when I launch because it's too short. And then there's a third line and I guess a second

41:02.416 --> 41:13.341
[SPEAKER_00]: line the second line goes from the camera also back to that same carabiner but it goes straight so when i am done flying or when i'm about

41:13.381 --> 41:22.888
[SPEAKER_00]: to land and i want it back in i'll release that little loop on the pulley system so that now the camera falls back down to where it's too low to get a

41:22.908 --> 41:31.452
[SPEAKER_00]: good shot i'm done shooting But it's long enough to make it all the way back to my harness. It's longer than my paragliding lines. And then I'll

41:31.572 --> 41:42.421
[SPEAKER_00]: pull in the little string that goes directly from the follow cam to my harness. And then I'll put it back in the box. So basically, it's some

41:42.481 --> 41:53.590
[SPEAKER_02]: kind of a triangle which goes between your lower surface and around the centers and around the C risers. That's right. Yes, it's a triangle. The

41:53.630 --> 42:00.253
[SPEAKER_00]: top part has a pulley so I can adjust the height of where it flies. Damn, that's smart, man. You should try and sell that system because I'm

42:00.274 --> 42:08.719
[SPEAKER_02]: pretty sure there are a lot of people out there looking for that kind of solutions. Got it. I think as we approach the end of this part of the

42:08.919 --> 42:18.486
[SPEAKER_02]: episode, one challenge or one new aspect from what I see that has come up with content creation is that short form has picked up so much in today's

42:18.547 --> 42:26.730
[SPEAKER_02]: world. We all know attention spans are reducing. Social media is to blame up to some extent and everything around it. But A lot of people are

42:26.870 --> 42:36.674
[SPEAKER_02]: just settling in for short-form content and long-form isn't really becoming that viable anymore. So do you have any advice for the budding

42:36.734 --> 42:45.798
[SPEAKER_02]: filmmakers or content creators? Of course, chasing hard, doing what makes sense to you is how it should be. But if you were to start now with all

42:45.818 --> 42:57.164
[SPEAKER_02]: of these dreams in today's world and in today's age, how can somebody try and mimic your rate of success with the current industry trends and make

42:57.204 --> 43:08.691
[SPEAKER_02]: sense with it? I don't know. In some ways, I feel like a bit of a dinosaur because when I look around me and I see the way people interact with...

43:09.588 --> 43:21.335
[SPEAKER_00]: media, especially young people, I don't feel like I would have done very well as a human being growing up today. And that's not to sound resigned.

43:22.136 --> 43:31.602
[SPEAKER_00]: I appreciate I am a dinosaur and dinosaurs don't understand that they're about to go extinct. I feel like there's a lot for me to learn to be

43:31.642 --> 43:45.694
[SPEAKER_00]: able to properly answer that question. But I feel at the same time that Short form is because it works because people need a distraction, but

43:45.734 --> 43:53.419
[SPEAKER_00]: they're really busy. So they don't, they want to lose themselves for five minutes because they have five minutes and they don't want to think

43:53.459 --> 44:02.565
[SPEAKER_00]: about what's going on in their life and what they need to do, clean up their room, pay their bills, apologize to this person or go and do this,

44:02.685 --> 44:09.450
[SPEAKER_00]: change the oil on their car, whatever it is that they don't want to think about that's in their head, but normally for five minutes. And so that

44:10.190 --> 44:19.999
[SPEAKER_00]: serves a purpose. And I feel like there's always been a solution for that, whether it was the radio, the television, and people using those

44:20.039 --> 44:30.868
[SPEAKER_00]: things for short periods of time. I appreciate, because of the way that this has happened, our attention spans, even as adults, have dwindled

44:31.549 --> 44:46.437
[SPEAKER_00]: significantly. But I think that the key to so many of the things that I've done being as successful as they were is that somehow finding a way to

44:46.497 --> 44:56.245
[SPEAKER_00]: really make people care, really make people care about it. And I actually can't even really take credit for realizing how important

44:56.285 --> 45:08.926
[SPEAKER_00]: that was. Going back to skateboarding across Canada, I, as well as three other young gentlemen put this together and I can't even take credit for

45:09.326 --> 45:15.448
[SPEAKER_00]: how it started because I got on board at the last minute as the documentary maker and essentially became the fourth skateboarder. We

45:15.468 --> 45:24.372
[SPEAKER_00]: did this for the Canadian Breast Cancer Society and we raised a ton of money for breast cancer and a lot of awareness for breast cancer

45:24.432 --> 45:33.457
[SPEAKER_00]: occurring more and more frequently in younger women who wouldn't have thought that they would be susceptible. And so there we are doing this

45:33.497 --> 45:43.025
[SPEAKER_00]: thing. We've got this theme, these pink ribbons, Canadian Breast Cancer Society. And I remember on a message board from Toronto, I had

45:43.085 --> 45:52.934
[SPEAKER_00]: published some content and some guy said, it was a nice comment from a friend of mine. He said, of course, Ben would be the one to skateboard

45:52.954 --> 46:03.021
[SPEAKER_00]: across Canada and somehow find a way to make people care about it. That's what he said. And I realized like, hey, he's right. But also, he's

46:03.041 --> 46:10.286
[SPEAKER_00]: looking at this and thinking, if I did this, nobody would care. Because truth be told, he could have also done it. He had the time. He had the

46:10.306 --> 46:23.114
[SPEAKER_00]: skill. He would have had the desire to do all of this stuff. But he didn't have a way to make anyone care about why he was doing it. And here, it just

46:23.154 --> 46:33.553
[SPEAKER_00]: fell into my lap. And then it became obvious to me after the fact. we were doing it for young women we were four 25 year old skateboarders

46:34.193 --> 46:42.656
[SPEAKER_00]: skateboarding across canada to support the health of young women's breasts there is no more tongue-in-cheek project that i could have

46:42.716 --> 46:54.440
[SPEAKER_00]: imagined being a part of but it really made people care it really made people think it really made people talk that i feel is the important part

46:54.540 --> 47:04.181
[SPEAKER_00]: of every project If we take a look at this last one, the story of the monarch butterfly, there's no way to tell that short form. Maybe there

47:04.241 --> 47:14.726
[SPEAKER_00]: is by someone else, but for me, that's a huge undertaking that they have to fly across the United States to Mexico and back again. And so in order

47:14.766 --> 47:23.420
[SPEAKER_00]: for me to tell that story, the only way to tell that is is long form, and I think that someone who sees the trailer for that film will recognize

47:23.460 --> 47:34.285
[SPEAKER_00]: that, whoa, this is huge. This isn't just someone going and flying above the clouds, and wow, that's pretty, and that has its place, and it will

47:34.325 --> 47:43.129
[SPEAKER_00]: only take a few minutes to showcase that. I do have content like that, but these real stories that really make you think and that really take you to

47:43.169 --> 47:52.769
[SPEAKER_00]: a new place like your podcasts, you can't get there in less than an hour. And I feel like it's important just to take a look at what we're doing and

47:52.789 --> 47:59.691
[SPEAKER_00]: to ask yourself, why is someone going to care? And if we can't answer that, then we need to keep thinking about that. But as soon as we figure

47:59.711 --> 48:09.654
[SPEAKER_00]: that out, that's when we can start to engage in telling a story that's going to ask someone for an hour of their life. Wow, man. It's like poetry

48:09.674 --> 48:17.637
[SPEAKER_02]: in motion when you put it across so beautifully. Because it's all true, and it all makes a lot of sense. It's just a choice of realizing it and

48:17.817 --> 48:28.980
